    "article_title":"Establishing Morning Routines",
    "article_content":"Learn how visual schedules can significantly reduce transition anxiety for your child during the busy morning rush",
    "sensory_tip1_title":"Sound (Auditory)",
    "sensory_tip1_content1":"• Noise reduction: Use high-quality noise-cancelling earmuffs or soft silicone earplugs in loud or echoey environments.",
    "sensory_tip1_content2":"• Calming sounds: Utilize white noise, pink noise, or ambient, soothing playlists during stressful moments or before bedtime.",
    "sensory_tip1_content3":"• Headphone pairing: If your child is sensitive to sound but dislikes how headphones feel, slowly desensitize them by pairing headphones with highly preferred activities (e.g., watching a favorite video).",
    "sensory_tip2_title":"Touch (Tactile)",
    "sensory_tip2_content1":"• Clothing modifications: Cut out scratchy tags, flip clothes inside out to hide seams, or opt for seamless, soft-knit clothing and Lycra undergarments.",
    "sensory_tip2_content2":"• Deep pressure: Offer comforting, deep-pressure input through weighted lap pads, weighted blankets, or tight, secure hugs.",
    "sensory_tip2_content3":"• Tactile play: Encourage hands-on play with non-food or food items to build tolerance to different textures. Examples include kinetic sand, playdough, dry beans, or water beads.",
    "sensory_tip3_title":"Balance and Movement (Vestibular & Proprioceptive)",
    "sensory_tip3_content1":"• Heavy work: Heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling gives muscles and joints feedback that helps the brain relax. Try wheelbarrow walking or carrying weighted objects.",
    "sensory_tip3_content2":"• Movement breaks: Incorporate a small indoor trampoline, a therapy ball to bounce on, or a sensory swing to give them the motion they crave safely.",
    "sensory_tip3_content3":"• Active seating: Use wobble stools or inflatable canoe chairs to allow movement while staying seated at a desk or table.",
    "sensory_tip4_title":"Sight (Visual)",
    "sensory_tip4_content1":"• Adjust lighting: Dim overhead fluorescent lights and utilize softer, warmer lamps, or string lights in specific zones.",
    "sensory_tip4_content2":"• Sensory go-bags: Always carry good-quality sunglasses and a wide-brimmed hat to protect against bright lights in public.",
    "sensory_tip4_content3":"• Visual calming: Use visually mesmerizing, calming tools like lava lamps, bubble walls, or DIY \"calm down\" bottles (glitter and water in a sealed jar).",
    "sensory_tip5_title":"Smell & Taste (Olfactory & Gustatory)",
    "sensory_tip5_content1":"• Masking triggers: If your child is overwhelmed by smells (like perfumes or cleaning supplies), apply a drop of a calming, preferred essential oil or lotion to a sweatband they can wear and sniff.",
    "sensory_tip5_content2":"• Oral sensory tools: Provide safe chewing options like chewable jewelry or chew toys to satisfy oral fixation.",
    "sensory_tip5_content3":"• Flavor exploration: Encourage food play and gradual exposure to strongly flavored or distinct temperature foods to help with aversions.",
    "sensory_tip6_title":"Create a \"Calm Corner\"",
    "sensory_tip6_content1":"• Designate a specific, quiet, and clutter-free space in your home featuring low lighting, pillows, and a weighted blanket. Allow your child to retreat to this space when they feel overwhelmed or just need to \"recharge\" their batteries.",
    "general_sensory_tip_title":"Supporting Sensory Needs",
    "general_sensory_tip_description":"Many children with autism are sensitive to sound, light, touch, or textures.",
    "general_sensory_tip_title1":"• Create a quiet, safe space at home.",
    "general_sensory_tip_title2":"• Allow headphones if noise is stressful.",
    "general_sensory_tip_title3":"• Respect food texture preferences.",
    "general_sensory_tip_title4":"• Introduce new sensations slowly.",
    "general_communication_tip_title":"Supporting Communication",
    "general_communication_tip_description":"Children with autism may communicate in different ways including words or gestures.",
    "general_communication_tip_title1":"• Get your child’s attention before speaking.",
    "general_communication_tip_title2":"• Speak slowly and clearly.",
    "general_communication_tip_title3":"• Use consistent words.",
    "general_communication_tip_title4":"• Allow extra time to respond.",
    "general_behavioural_tip_title":"Understanding Behaviour",
    "general_behavioural_tip_description":"Behaviour is a form of communication for children with autism. Challenging behaviour often means the child is feeling overwhelmed, confused, or unable to express needs.",
    "general_behavioural_tip_title1":"• Observe behaviour patterns carefully.",
    "general_behavioural_tip_title2":"• Look for reasons behind the behaviour.",
    "general_behavioural_tip_title3":"• Remember that behaviour is not intentional misbehaviour.",
    "general_behavioural_tip_title4":"• Focus on understanding, not punishment.",
    "communication_tip1_title":"Speak directly and literally:",
    "communication_tip1_content":"Avoid sarcasm, idioms, or abstract concepts. Use short, clear phrases and tell them exactly what you want them to do, rather than what not to do.",
    "communication_tip2_title":"Allow processing time:",
    "communication_tip2_content":"It can take autistic children longer to process spoken information. Ask your question or give your instruction, then wait at least 10 seconds before speaking again.",
    "communication_tip3_title":"Use visual supports:",
    "communication_tip3_content":"Many autistic children are highly visual learners. Pair your words with pointing, gestures, or use visual schedules and picture boards (like PECS) to help them understand daily routines and choices.",
    "communication_tip4_title":"Follow their lead:",
    "communication_tip4_content":"Instead of interrupting their focus to prompt speech, join in and comment on what they are doing. This builds trust and shared attention.",
    "communication_tip5_title":"Offer clear choices:",
    "communication_tip5_content": "Open-ended questions (e.g., \"What do you want to do?\") can be overwhelming. Instead, offer a clear, limited set of physical choices (e.g., \"Do you want the red cup or the blue cup?\").",
    "communication_tip6_title":"Accept all forms of communication:",
    "communication_tip6_content":" Speech is only one way to communicate. Be responsive to gestures, sounds, pointing, and Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) devices.",
    "behavioural_tip1_title":"Identify the Root Cause",
    "behavioural_tip1_content1":"• Observe Triggers: Figure out what happens immediately before a behavior. Is the child trying to avoid a task, seeking sensory input, or feeling overstimulated in a noisy environment?",
    "behavioural_tip1_content2":"• Address Sensory Needs: If a child becomes overwhelmed, provide a safe, calm-down space with sensory tools like weighted blankets or noise-canceling headphones.",
    "behavioural_tip2_title":"Communicate Clearly & Predictably",
    "behavioural_tip2_content1":"• Use Visual Schedules: Use pictures or a timeline to help the child understand daily routines and transitions, which reduces anxiety.",
    "behavioural_tip2_content2":"• Give \"First/Then\" Statements: Instead of saying \"stop playing,\" say, \"First we finish our lunch, then we can play\".",
    "behavioural_tip2_content3":"• Limit \"Don'ts\": Phrase instructions positively. Instead of \"Don't run,\" try \"Walk on the sidewalk\".",
    "behavioural_tip3_title":"Foster Agency and Success",
    "behavioural_tip3_content1":"• Offer Controlled Choices: Give a sense of control by offering limited, acceptable choices (e.g., \"Do you want to wear the red or blue shirt?\").",
    "behavioural_tip3_content2":"• Chunk Tasks: Break down overwhelming tasks (like homework) into small, manageable steps.",
    "behavioural_tip4_title":"Apply Positive Reinforcement",
    "behavioural_tip4_content1":"• Catch Them Being Good: Praise specific positive behaviors immediately. (e.g., \"I love how nicely you are sitting at the table!\")",
    "behavioural_tip4_content1":"• Catch Them Being Good: Praise specific positive behaviors immediately. (e.g., \"I love how nicely you are sitting at the table!\")",
    "behavioural_tip4_content2": "• Provide Breaks: Teach your child to request a break using a specific cue or \"Break\" card to regroup when frustrated.",
